  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1110 PM CST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air analysis late Friday \nevening show an amplified synoptic pattern is place atop the CONUS, \nwith deep, longwave troughing extending from the west coast eastward \nthrough the High Plains and into the upper Midwest, and longwave \nridging centered over Dixie Alley. At the surface, broad low \npressure reaches from south-central Canada all the way southward \ninto the southern plains, which is fostering strong southerly winds \nacross southwest KS. Daytime Saturday, the upstream longwave trough \nwill move hastily eastward, with an embedded vorticity max ejecting \nonto the southern/central plains by 00Z Sunday. Ahead of these \nfeatures, a strong cold front will initially drift southward into \nour area before accelerating as the strongest upper forcing arrives. \nThe primary concern with this evolution will be the potential for \nsevere convection along/ahead of the front. Between a strong upper \nlevel wave and southerly winds at the surface, deep layer shear will \nbe considerable, on the order of 40-50 kts. Alternatively, very poor \nlapse rates of less than 7.5 C/km will severely limit instability \nand result in most updrafts being sheared apart. Therefore, SPC's \nmarginal risk outlined across our far southeast zones appears \njustified, with strong wind gusts the only realistic threat. \nOtherwise, strong northerly winds in the wake of the front will draw \nmuch cooler air equatorward, fostering sub-freezing Sunday morning \nlows for roughly the northwestern half of our area while areas in \nthe southeast half drop into the mid to upper 30s.\n\nDaytime Sunday, surface high pressure will settle into the central \nplains in the wake of Saturday's cold front, aided by weak shortwave \nridging aloft. Northerly winds through the day along with below \nnormal afternoon highs in the 40s will feel fairly cold relative to \nthe anomalous warmth southwest KS has enjoyed the past few weeks. \nThe first widespread hard freeze is likely Sunday night/monday \nmorning as light winds and clear skies allow temperatures to bottom \nout in the low to mid 20s. This is well-past the climatological \nfirst hard freeze date, so no freeze warnings will be issued.\n\nMonday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles suggest \nthe cool weather will be here to stay as modestly low heights remain \natop the central plains. NBM highs through the end of the upcoming \nbusiness week are mainly in the 40s, although temperatures may \nstruggle to break out of the 30s on Tuesday. Precipitation chances \nthrough the week will be sparse, with Tuesday currently the only \nopportunity as both the ENS and GEFS eject an open upper level wave \nonto the central plains. These open waves rarely produce anything \nsubstantial for southwest KS, which is reflected by NBM pops only in \nthe slight chance (15-24%) to low-end chance (25-54%) categories, so \nexpectations should be limited.",
